  "structuredPassagePages": [
    {
      "width": 1489,
      "height": 2106,
      "label": "Reading passage page 1",
      "lines": [
        {
          "x": 231,
          "y": 135,
          "width": 107,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "PART A",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 197,
          "width": 938,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "Read Text] and answer questions 1-24 on pages 1-3 of the Question-Answer Book for Part A.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 256,
          "width": 59,
          "height": 18,
          "text": "Text 1",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 415,
          "y": 321,
          "width": 711,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "In from the cold among warm-hearted Koreans",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 234,
          "y": 377,
          "width": 1069,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "{1] Ten years ago, Oxford University graduate Daniel Tudor moved to Seoul, preferring the warmth of",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 232,
          "y": 406,
          "width": 1068,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "Korean society to “cold” Britain. The 31-year-old has since authored two books on his adopted home and",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 231,
          "y": 434,
          "width": 1069,
          "height": 26,
          "text": "has several other volumes in the pipeline. He speaks to Charmaine Chan about his latest title, A Geek in",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 232,
          "y": 465,
          "width": 285,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "Korea, due out in June 2014.",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 539,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 24,
          "text": "You started, like so many other Westerners in Korea, teaching English. When did you begin writing",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 569,
          "width": 240,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "about Korea and why?",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 231,
          "y": 601,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 33,
          "text": "[2] When I joined The Economist [2010-2013] I thought, “Eventually I’d like to write a book about Korea",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 635,
          "width": 390,
          "height": 24,
          "text": "because nobody else is really doing it.”",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 711,
          "width": 201,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "Why weren’t they?",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 747,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "{3] Korea is a bit off the radar for most people in Western countries. In the 1980s Japan was the big story",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 181,
          "y": 750,
          "width": 20,
          "height": 18,
          "text": "10",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 776,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "and people pay attention to China now because of its huge population and market. Korea has fallen in",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 806,
          "width": 286,
          "height": 18,
          "text": "between these two countries.",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 876,
          "width": 973,
          "height": 32,
          "text": "In your book, you write about jeong the invisible hug. Is that something exclusive to Korea?",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 913,
          "width": 1069,
          "height": 33,
          "text": "[4] A lot of Koreans say jeong — the warmth between people and mutual sacrifice — is uniquely Korean, as",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 946,
          "width": 1071,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "is han. It’s nonsense, but Korea has words to describe these things, which shows they are important. Han",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 181,
          "y": 949,
          "width": 19,
          "height": 17,
          "text": "5",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 975,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "is a burden, oppression or an injustice you can’t correct. Its cause never goes away but you can temporarily",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 1003,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 26,
          "text": "forget about it by pursuing all-out, manic fun. This is where heung comes in. Heung is pure joy. The word",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 1032,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 22,
          "text": "isn’t as famous as han, but I think that it should be. Even traditional Korean funerals used to feature",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 1061,
          "width": 597,
          "height": 24,
          "text": "extreme alcohol consumption, raucous singing, and the like.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 1134,
          "width": 1069,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "[5] Often when Westerners think of East Asians, the stereotypes of stoicism and self-control — the so-",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 178,
          "y": 1137,
          "width": 22,
          "height": 17,
          "text": "20",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 1163,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "called “inscrutable oriental” — come to mind. But Koreans in fact tend to be very expressive and open with",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1192,
          "width": 848,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "their feelings. Somehow, sadness and happiness both seem to be magnified in Korea.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1268,
          "width": 490,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "How is A Geek different from your first book?",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 1304,
          "width": 1069,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "[6]. It’s still about South Korea, but it’s aimed at a younger audience. Consider it a gateway for those who",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 1334,
          "width": 807,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "like K-pop or TV shows from Korea, but don’t know anything about the country.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 177,
          "y": 1337,
          "width": 22,
          "height": 17,
          "text": "25",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 1409,
          "width": 572,
          "height": 24,
          "text": "A few chapters are about K-pop. Are you into K-pop?",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 1445,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 27,
          "text": "[7] Generally K-pop is for teenagers. I’m not saying it’s wrong. It’s a good business. But I like music",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1474,
          "width": 1072,
          "height": 27,
          "text": "played by people who mean what they’re writing. Some people think all Korean music is K-pop, but",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1504,
          "width": 1072,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "there’s really good music in Korea that’s not superficial or played on the radio or on TV and doesn’t go",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 1532,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "outside of Korea. One of my favourite bands is 3rd Line Butterfly: these guys are not rich and famous;",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 178,
          "y": 1536,
          "width": 22,
          "height": 18,
          "text": "30",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1561,
          "width": 1071,
          "height": 26,
          "text": "they’re ordinary guys you can be friends with. | am friends with them. There’s an interview with",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 1590,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 27,
          "text": "[Gangnam Style singer] Psy [in Geek]. He’s funny and cheeky, in a Robbie Williams kind of way, and",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1619,
          "width": 863,
          "height": 26,
          "text": "making fun of Gangnam [an affluent district of Seoul], which is superficial and flashy.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1697,
          "width": 279,
          "height": 22,
          "text": "How about Korean soaps?",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 1732,
          "width": 1069,
          "height": 26,
          "text": "[8] 1 don’t like the drama stuff. They’re trying to play with your emotions with Cinderella stories:",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 178,
          "y": 1736,
          "width": 21,
          "height": 18,
          "text": "35",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1761,
          "width": 1072,
          "height": 26,
          "text": "beautiful girl from poor family marries rich guy. Korea’s probably not the best country in which to be a",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1790,
          "width": 1072,
          "height": 26,
          "text": "woman. If you’re a young woman in Korea, what’s the best way to become wealthy or to achieve status?",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 1820,
          "width": 304,
          "height": 24,
          "text": "Sadly, it’s to marry somebody.",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1996,
          "width": 589,
          "height": 20,
          "text": "2015-DSE-ENG LANG 1-A-RP-2 2",
          "bold": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "width": 1489,
      "height": 2106,
      "label": "Reading passage page 2",
      "lines": [
        {
          "x": 235,
          "y": 131,
          "width": 802,
          "height": 19,
          "text": "You also write about how Koreans are obsessed with success and education.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 182,
          "y": 189,
          "width": 24,
          "height": 18,
          "text": "40",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 235,
          "y": 189,
          "width": 1069,
          "height": 24,
          "text": "[9] You find these mothers in Gangnam and they’re scary. When I taught English I’d meet kids who,",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 234,
          "y": 217,
          "width": 1071,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "materially, led awesome lives and they’d show up in these big Mercedes with bags as big as they were. But",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 234,
          "y": 246,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "if they didn’t get an A grade in something, their parents would get mad and the next time you saw them",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 233,
          "y": 275,
          "width": 1071,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "they’d be crying. Wealthy families are obsessed with education. It’s a status thing: preserve your status and",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 234,
          "y": 304,
          "width": 896,
          "height": 24,
          "text": "show the rest of the world that you’re preserving your status and your kids are doing well.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 181,
          "y": 379,
          "width": 23,
          "height": 18,
          "text": "45",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 234,
          "y": 379,
          "width": 407,
          "height": 22,
          "text": "Why do you continue to live in Korea?",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 234,
          "y": 412,
          "width": 1069,
          "height": 33,
          "text": "[10] This jeong stuff — that’s the thing that keeps me in Korea. Korea made me a better friend to my",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 233,
          "y": 444,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 26,
          "text": "friends. England’s a cold society and, growing up, I suppose I always wanted this feeling of being",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 233,
          "y": 473,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "connected to people. I thought English people were a bit too cynical and cold. Korea is a place where you",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 232,
          "y": 502,
          "width": 588,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "say, “I like you. I love you. This is great.” I really like that.",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 231,
          "y": 676,
          "width": 921,
          "height": 33,
          "text": "Read Text 2 and answer questions 25-31 on page 4 of the Question-Answer Book for Part A.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 231,
          "y": 738,
          "width": 61,
          "height": 18,
          "text": "Text 2",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 371,
          "y": 804,
          "width": 786,
          "height": 32,
          "text": "Tudor’s Book Covers Implausible, Impossible Korea",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 862,
          "width": 171,
          "height": 22,
          "text": "October 15, 2012",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 891,
          "width": 178,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "By Evan Ramstad",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 231,
          "y": 960,
          "width": 1069,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "[1] Daniel Tudor is one of the most influential foreign correspondents in South Korea — and one of the",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 989,
          "width": 1071,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "least known. As the reporter for the Economist, which doesn’t use bylines, most of his work is published",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 1018,
          "width": 1071,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "anonymously. But Mr. Tudor’s profile is about to take a sharp rise with the publication of his new book,",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 1047,
          "width": 342,
          "height": 22,
          "text": "“Korea: The Impossible Country”.",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 230,
          "y": 1104,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "[2] It’s the first English-language book to cover the whole waterfront of South Korean society — historical,",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1133,
          "width": 1072,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "cultural, economic, social, political — since one by another influential British expat, Michael Breen, with",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1162,
          "width": 1073,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "“The Koreans,” which was originally published in 1998 and revised in 2004. [Mr. Breen provided a",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1191,
          "width": 1072,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "recommendation on Mr. Tudor’s book jacket.] “Korea: The Impossible Country” is also likely to get added",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 227,
          "y": 1219,
          "width": 1072,
          "height": 24,
          "text": "to the list of must-read books for anyone from outside of South Korea who wants to do business or live in",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 227,
          "y": 1248,
          "width": 116,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "the country.",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 229,
          "y": 1307,
          "width": 1070,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "[3] That’s a small canon, unfortunately. In addition to Mr. Breen’s book, the other indispensables are",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 227,
          "y": 1337,
          "width": 1072,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "“Diamond Dilemma” by Tariq Hussain, “Korean Dynasty” by Donald Kirk, Tom Coyner’s guide to doing",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 227,
          "y": 1365,
          "width": 1072,
          "height": 26,
          "text": "business in Korea and Robert Koehler’s Seoul Selection guidebooks for places and sightseeing. Indeed, the",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1394,
          "width": 553,
          "height": 24,
          "text": "list of must-read books about North Korea is far longer.",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 228,
          "y": 1453,
          "width": 1074,
          "height": 25,
          "text": "[4] Mr. Tudor pushes into new social and economic territory with his book, including the rising role of",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 179,
          "y": 1454,
          "width": 19,
          "height": 17,
          "text": "15",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
